b) Don't lock; auto-fire after a timeout; if user scrolls before shooting, auto-fire.</p><p>Best UX: (a) lock + auto-fire fallback after ~7s, plus cinema-mode auto-fire immediately, plus reduced-motion skip. If the user scrolls during the lock... lenis stopped means wheel does nothing (smooth scroll disabled) — but native scroll can still happen via keyboard/scrollbar. The start gate used lenis.stop() while the gate overlay was up; here the section is pinned — if the user force-scrolls (scrollbar drag), ScrollTrigger would advance into the pinned timeline. To handle that gracefully, I can structure the timeline so that scroll progress BEFORE the shot just doesn't do anything meaningful (the archery overlay is not scrubbed — it's a real-time interactive layer), and after the shot, a scrubbed timeline drives the tree sequence. Also auto-fire when the user scrolls hard (wheel event while locked → auto-fire the shot instead of blocking). That's elegant: wheel/touch attempts → triggers the auto-shot.</p><p>Hmm, but wait — careful: touch scroll attempts (touchstart) on mobile would trigger auto-fire — good.</p><p>Actually, let me reconsider: is locking necessary? What if the scene is: pin starts, archery interactive overlay active. The scrubbed timeline is PAUSED at 0 until the shot happens. Scroll events while unshot → ignore (lenis stopped + ScrollTrigger still fires but timeline is paused at 0 and I clamp). Simplest robust approach:</p><ul class="list-inside list-disc whitespace-normal" data-streamdown="unordered-list">
