"color: rgb(36, 41, 46); --shiki-dark: #E1E4E8;">  </span><span class="dark:bg-(--shiki-dark-bg)! dark:text-(--shiki-dark)!" style="color: rgb(106, 115, 125); --shiki-dark: #6A737D;">// render loop: own rAF gated by active flag; time uniform advances (petal animation is time-based)</span></span><span class="block before:content-[counter(line)] before:inline-block before:[counter-increment:line] before:w-4 before:mr-4 before:text-[13px] before:text-right before:text-muted-foreground/50 before:font-mono before:select-none"><span class="dark:bg-(--shiki-dark-bg)! dark:text-(--shiki-dark)!" style="color: rgb(36, 41, 46); --shiki-dark: #E1E4E8;">})();</span></span><span class="block before:content-[counter(line)] before:inline-block before:[counter-increment:line] before:w-4 before:mr-4 before:text-[13px] before:text-right before:text-muted-foreground/50 before:font-mono before:select-none"></span></code></pre></div><p>Wait — petals animation in the original is purely time-driven (t += delta), NOT progress. The petals spiral upward continuously — an ambient system. Progress controls: camera path (orbit in, rise), overall visibility (fade in), petal density (bloom uniform — hmm, the original has no density control; petals always all active). For the growth feel: I'll add uniforms: <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm" data-streamdown="inline-code">uReveal</code> (petals alpha global), and tree material opacity. Camera: azimuth from -0.6π → 0.35π, height 1.5→7, radius 13→8.5, target (0, 3.5→4.5, 0). Also gentle auto-drift always (time * 0.02) so it feels alive even while not scrolling. Pointer parallax: azimuth += pointer.x * 0.15, polar slight.</p><p>The 2026 effect: needs <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm" data-streamdown="inline-code">azimuth</code> uniform = camera azimuthal angle (in original via OrbitControls.getAzimuthalAngle()). I compute it myself from camera position: Math.atan2(x, z). The effect box rotates with camera azimuth. Keep.</p><p>The "2026" texture: canvas 128x64, text "2026". In the original, effect petals turn bright red (0.95, 0, 0.45). For night theme: gold! (0.98, 0.78, 0.35) with bigger size — the year glows GOLD in petals. Lovely against pink petals. Actually the original effect color mix: <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm" data-streamdown="inline-code">mix(diffuseColor.rgb, vec3(0.95,0,0.45), vIsEffect)</code> — I'll use gold (1.0, 0.82, 0.42).</p><p>Petals colors: original near-white warm (0xffffcc base with variation) and pink-ish center (1, 0.7, 0.9) mix. Night theme: keep warm whites/pinks (they read well on dark sky) — maybe slightly more pink/rose: base #ffd9e4-ish... The vertex colors: c.set(0xffffcc); color = (r, g - rand<em>0.1, b + rand</em>0.2) — warm cream. Fine on dark. Tree points color 0xffbbff (light pink) size 0.25 — fine.</p><p>Base disc: original pink #ff0088 with radial mix to clear color. Night: make it a dark warm pool — actually a soft ROSE/GOLD glowing disc under the tree like moonlight hitting grass: radial gradient center rgba rose → edge transparent-dark. The shader mixes outgoingLight to bc color by radius — I'll set bc to a deep plum (#1a1026) and add slight additive glow? MeshBasicMaterial with the radial mix to a dark plum edge... on transparent canvas, edges should be... the base disc is opaque geometry; on a transparent canvas over the site sky, an opaque disc is fine (it's the "ground" the tree stands on). Style: deep violet plum disc with rose-gold rim glow. I'll modify the fragment: col = mix(outgoingLight*?, ...). Keep structure, choose colors: center #2a1638 (dark) with a subtle warm rim... Actually simpler: keep the original formula (mix(outgoingLight, bc, length(uv))) with baseMat color 0x30203f and bc deep night #0d0a18 → a disc that fades INTO the site's night sky at edges. Add a soft gold ring glow at the rim via another ring mesh with additive material. Keep simple first, tune later.*</p><p>Also floating petals that FALL near the end? The original spirals up. Keep up.</p><p>Growth feel: at p=0 tree points opacity 0 → tree invisible; bloom in by p 0.05→0.25 (points appear with size ramp). Petal system alpha 0 until 0.15, full by 0.35. 2026 effect intensity: uniform scale — original has isEffect always on; I'll add uYear uniform 0→1 to lerp the effect in around p 0.35→0.5 (right before wish text). Nice.</p><p>Camera: I'll write a small update(progress, time) — position on a spiral: angle = lerp(-2.2, 0.9, easedP) + time*0.03 (slow drift); radius = lerp(14, 8.5, p); height = lerp(2.2, 6.2, p); lookAt target (0, lerp(3,5,p), 0).</p><p><span class="font-semibold" data-streamdown="strong">Performance</span>: 15k + 1250 points, custom shaders — cheap. DPR cap 1.75. Pause when inactive (ScrollTrigger onToggle on section). Also mobile 6500 petals.</p><p><span class="font-semibold" data-streamdown="strong">WebGL fallback</span>: try/catch renderer creation → if fail, sec.classList.add("no-tree") → CSS shows a static radial glow + the wish text still plays (text is DOM). Archery still works. Fine.</p><p><span class="font-semibold" data-streamdown="strong">tree.obj</span>: download to assets/models/tree.obj. Let me check its size and content format (need v/f only). If it has vt/vn — handle. OBJ parser: read lines, collect v (x,y,z), f (indices possibly a/b/c or a//b or a) — triangulate fan. Then surface sampler: standard area-weighted triangle sampling. ~60 lines.</p><p>Actually, WAIT — maybe simpler and even better: skip OBJ entirely, generate a PROCEDURAL tree point cloud? The tree.obj silhouette is nice though, and user asked "jaisa Blossom zip mein" — the OBJ-based tree IS the look. Download the OBJ (it's a three.js example asset, MIT-licensed repo). Let me check size — curl showed 200 but no content-length in the headers I got (only first 5 lines). Let me download and see. If &lt; 200KB, vendor it. (e2b has network, we cloned github fine.)</p><p><span class="font-semibold" data-streamdown="strong">Archery module</span> (js/archery.js classic script):</p><p>Port from gift: setDraw/refreshRig/applyNock/shotGeom/miniHeart+burstHearts/startBeat/stopBeat/fire/springBack/autoFire + pointer/keyboard handlers + enter reveal timeline + my custom shot timeline (arrow flight → strike → burst → bloom wash). Elements: I'll keep gift ids (target, archery, bow, arrow, strL, strR, serving, tip, aim) — check collisions with main project ids: grep for these in index.html... The main project uses ids: gate, startBtn, watchBtn, loader, rail, audioBtn, pxScroll, yearsRig, rig (roadRig), rig #rig (corridor old), memoriesLine, garland, cGrid, houseCanvas, houseHotspots, houseKicker... "rig"! The old corridor had <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm" data-streamdown="inline-code">&lt;div class="rig" id="rig"&gt;</code> — that section is being replaced, so gone. "target"? not used. "aim"? no. OK but to be safe I'll prefix all with <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm" data-streamdown="inline-code">bw-</code> (bow-): bw-target, bw-archery, bw-bow, bw-arrow, bw-strL, bw-strR, bw-serving, bw-tip, bw-aim, bw-hint, bw-eyebrow, bw-heart, bw-stage, bw-burst. SVG defs ids (gradients: hg, hsheen, limb, limbHi, grip, shaft, gold, feath, wing) — gift index has gradient ids like "hg" — check main index.html for those ids: the main project has SVG defs (scene1/scene2/scene3 groups — an SVG somewhere with gradients?). grep needed. I'll prefix gradient ids too: bw-hg, bw-hsheen, bw-limb... Safe.</p><p>The gift's shot timeline (buildFilm) I'm partially porting: from release → arrow flies (0→0.26), strike + burst (0.26→0.6), heart falls (0.64→1.05), flood circle (1.0→1.34), then MY handoff: instead of gift's field/kinetic type, the flood washes → fades revealing the blossom tree (Blossom.reveal() → canvas fade + camera at p=0 + lenis.start() + scroll nudge). Also a "bloom" gold radial wash element (gift