g><svg width="1.5em" height="1.5em" stroke-width="1.5" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" color="currentColor" class="text-text-secondary hidden h-3.5 w-3.5 shrink-0 transition-transform duration-200 group-hover:block"><path d="M9 6L15 12L9 18" stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"></path></svg><p class="leading-[normal]">Thought for 10 seconds</p></button><div class="text-text-secondary text-sm" style="overflow: clip; height: 0px; opacity: 0; filter: blur(2px);"><div style="overflow: auto;"><div class="relative pl-6 pr-2 pt-4" style="will-change: transform;"><div class="bg-sidebar-border absolute bottom-0 left-[6px] top-4 w-0.5 rounded-full"></div><div class="space-y-4 whitespace-normal size-full [&amp;&gt;*:first-child]:mt-0 [&amp;&gt;*:last-child]:mb-0 font-mono text-xs leading-relaxed"><p>Parallax.js is clean — no scroll manipulation. So the freeze isn't from scene code. The freeze points (5401, 6040) with target=anim=y synced means a native scroll event occurred mid-animation — i.e., something called a native scroll OR the browser scrolled itself.</p><p><span class="font-semibold" data-streamdown="strong"><code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm" data-streamdown="inline-code">window.scrollTo(0, 5401)</code>-style behavior</span> — WHAT in ScrollTrigger does this? <span class="font-semibold" data-streamdown="strong"><code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm" data-streamdown="inline-code">ScrollTrigger.refresh()</code></span> — during refresh, ScrollTrigger: 1) unpins everything, 2) measures, 3) re-pins — and to avoid visual jumps it RECORDS scroll and RESTORES it — the restore is <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm" data-streamdown="inline-code">window.scrollTo</code> or <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm" data-streamdown="inline-code">scrollTo</code> fallback... <span class="font-semibold" data-streamdown="strong">If a refresh happens mid-lenis-animation</span>: refresh saves scroll (say 4200), unpin/repin changes layout, restores 4200 — meanwhile lenis continues animating to its target 6500... the final position would still be 6500 unless...</p><p>Hmm wait — but lenis's target got synced DOWN to 5401 — meaning lenis SAW a native scroll at 5401 (its onNativeScroll). If refresh's restore landed at 5401 while lenis was animating... lenis's onNativeScroll fires only when <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm" data-streamdown="inline-code">isScrolling === false || 'native'</code> — during animation isScrolling is... hmm lenis's animate is running so isScrolling truthy → onNativeScroll IGNORED?? Then target stays 6500 and animation continues... but evidence: target=5401.</p><p>You know what — I keep going deeper into speculation. Let me check WHO calls ScrollTrigger.refresh() during my scroll: add a probe hooking <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm" data-streamdown="inline-code">ScrollTrigger.addEventListener('refreshInit'/'refresh')</code> with console.log + capture stack, and also hook window.scrollTo + element.scrollTo + scrollTop setter to log stacks. THEN scroll and see the culprit.</p><p>Actually — WAIT. One more candidate I haven't considered: <span class="font-semibold" data-streamdown="strong">the <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm" data-streamdown="inline-code">resize</code> listener in main.js</span> (<code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm" data-streamdown="inline-code">addEventListener("resize", ...)</code> → debounced ScrollTrigger.refresh). Headless browser viewport is fixed... BUT — <span class="font-semibold" data-streamdown="strong"><code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm" data-streamdown="inline-code">ScrollTrigger</code> itself auto-refreshes on <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm" data-streamdown="inline-cod