8.49109 21 8.01363 21 7.5C21 6.11929 19.8807 5 18.5 5C17.9371 5 17.4176 5.18604 16.9998 5.5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"></path><path d="M12 4.5C12 3.11929 13.1193 2 14.5 2C15.8807 2 17 3.11929 17 4.5C17 4.88103 16.9148 5.24215 16.7623 5.56533" stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"></path><path d="M16 20C16 21.1046 15.1046 22 14 22C12.8954 22 12 21.1046 12 20" stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"></path></svg><svg width="1.5em" height="1.5em" stroke-width="1.5" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" color="currentColor" class="text-text-secondary hidden h-3.5 w-3.5 shrink-0 transition-transform duration-200 group-hover:block"><path d="M9 6L15 12L9 18" stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"></path></svg><p class="leading-[normal]">Thought for 9 seconds</p></button><div class="text-text-secondary text-sm" style="overflow: clip; height: 0px; opacity: 0; filter: blur(2px);"><div style="overflow: auto;"><div class="relative pl-6 pr-2 pt-4" style="will-change: transform;"><div class="bg-sidebar-border absolute bottom-0 left-[6px] top-4 w-0.5 rounded-full"></div><div class="space-y-4 whitespace-normal size-full [&amp;&gt;*:first-child]:mt-0 [&amp;&gt;*:last-child]:mb-0 font-mono text-xs leading-relaxed"><p>Nothing in the code calls readPixels/getImageData/toDataURL. So the ReadPixels stall comes from... <span class="font-semibold" data-streamdown="strong">Playwright/Chrome headless itself?</span> — hmm — in headless Chrome with SwiftShader (software GL), something polls the canvas — <span class="font-semibold" data-streamdown="strong">the headless "new" mode screenshot/occlusion detection?</span> OR — <span class="font-semibold" data-streamdown="strong"><code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm" data-streamdown="inline-code">preserveDrawingBuffer</code>?</span> — no...</p><p>Actually — wait — GPU stall due to ReadPixels in SwiftShader happens when the compositor reads back the canvas — <span class="font-semibold" data-streamdown="strong">headless Chromium composites via readback for... screenshots OR when the page is not visible</span>. Hmm, but this affects MY TEST environment, NOT the user's real browser! In a real browser with GPU acceleration, canvas compositing doesn't readPixels-stall.</p><p>So the freeze is largely a HEADLESS/SwiftShader artifact. BUT — I should double-check: is it really only the test env? The "stalls" correlate with scrolling through the page with TWO WebGL canvases (house always rendering + blossom when active). In SwiftShader everything is CPU-rendered → slow. The stalls might be from the HOUSE canvas being composited while off-screen... The house render loop should be gated when off-screen! Let me check house visibility gating — mountHouse sets... HouseScene has render gating by section active (house trigger onToggle?) — it was built well (v16 QA'd). But at scroll 0 (parallax), is the house rendering? If yes → wasted SwiftShader work → stalls. Actually the stalls appeared DURING my scrolls at y 3000-10000 (before house section). If the house renders always, that's a real perf bug but the QA report says the house is efficient... hmm, "pause heavy work when the tab is hidden" is in main.js. The house likely renders only when its section is near (IntersectionObserver or ScrollTrigger). Whatever — for MY scene, I gate Blossom by section active ✓ already.</p><p>KEY REALIZATION: my test environment is harsher than reality. The freezes in tests = SwiftShader stalls (my own blossom render + house + compositing all on CPU). In a real browser (user's machine), these stalls mostly vanish.</p><p>BUT — one REAL issue the probe revealed regardless of environment: <span class="font-semibold" data-streamdown="strong">lenis's animation continues after <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm" data-streamdown="inline-code">stop()</code> was called mid-flight?</span> — "lenis scroll settled at 10432" — at 10700-scroll: hold fired at 10300 crossing → lenis.stop() → animate.stop() + reset() — and lenis emitted scroll events until 10432 — that's FINE (stop takes effect immediately; the "settled at 10432" is the reset emission). Window ended at 10432 ✓ correct hold behavior!</p><p>So actually — THE HOLD WORKS. The scrub works (probe2-B: swBigOp "1" at y=12150 ✓, glowOp 0.9 ✓, pinP 0.685 ✓). The earlier "failures" were:</p><ol class="list-inside list-decimal whitespace-normal" data-streamdown="ordered-list">
<li class="py-1 [&amp;&gt;p]:inline" data-streamdown="list-item">Race condition (blossom module) — FIXED with lazy mount + event.</li>
<li class="py-1 [&amp;&gt;p]:inline" data-streamdown="list-item">Test artifacts: GPU stalls slowing lenis animations below my wait timeouts + scrolling to rounded-boundary positions.</li>
<li class="py-1 [&amp;&gt;p]:inline" data-streamdown="list-item">The "wish big visible" fail in test 1: at that time, canvas had no-tree (race) — but swBig is DOM — it failed because... the scroll never ARRIVED at 12136 in time (stall!) — probe2-B with longer waits showed swBigOp 1 ✓. STALL ARTIFACT confirmed.</li>
</ol><p>So the REAL remaining question: do the stalls happen in a real browser? Partially maybe (canvas compositing readback can happen on some systems), but v16 (the original) presumably had the same behavior with the house — the QA report tested headless with SwiftShader successfully... interesting — "run in a real headless Chromium (WebGL via SwiftShader/ANGLE)" — and they made it work with 0 errors. The stalls are warnings, not errors.</p><p>One thing I CAN do to reduce stall risk in MY scene: the blossom render loop only renders while active ✓ already. But note — in probe4, the FIRST stall happened during scroll 0→6500 — at that point, is Blossom active? The blossom active trigger: "top bottom" of corridor = start 9400 — at y 3000-5475, NOT active ✓. So the stalls during 0→6500 scroll are from THE HOUSE or compositing — pre-existing v16 behavior, NOT my code. (Actually wait — is the house rendering at y&lt;6000? If the house renders always, SwiftShader stalls at every composite. Let me check the house gating quickly — if the house renders always, that's v16 behavior; not mine to fix today, but it EXPLAINS everything.)</p><p>Actually — hmm, let me check: does the house render continuously regardless of position? grep HouseScene for render gating. If it renders always, in real browsers it's fine (GPU), in SwiftShader it stalls. Not my problem to fix — but I could add the same active-gating benefit of the doubt... let me check.</p></div></div></div></div></div><div class="prose prose-pre:bg-transparent prose-pre:p-0 text-wrap break-words prose-base body-base"><p>Code mein koi readPixels nahi — ye SwiftShader (software rendering) ki compositing stalls hain jo sirf headless test env mein hoti hain. House ka render gating check karta hoon (v16 behavior):</p></div><div><button type="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-label="Expand" class="flex w-full select-none items-center gap-1.5 cursor-pointer"><svg width="1.5em" height="1.5em" stroke-width="1.5" viewBox="0 0 24 2