condary hidden h-3.5 w-3.5 shrink-0 transition-transform duration-200 group-hover:block"><path d="M9 6L15 12L9 18" stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"></path></svg><p class="leading-[normal]">Thought for 18 seconds</p></button><div class="text-text-secondary text-sm" style="overflow: clip; height: 0px; opacity: 0; filter: blur(2px);"><div style="overflow: auto;"><div class="relative pl-6 pr-2 pt-4" style="will-change: transform;"><div class="bg-sidebar-border absolute bottom-0 left-[6px] top-4 w-0.5 rounded-full"></div><div class="space-y-4 whitespace-normal size-full [&amp;&gt;*:first-child]:mt-0 [&amp;&gt;*:last-child]:mb-0 font-mono text-xs leading-relaxed"><p>The house has extensive optimization (visibility zones, etc.) but likely still renders its loop... line 223 <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm" data-streamdown="inline-code">if (!visible) return;</code> in index.js — some gating exists. OK — the stalls are environmental (SwiftShader compositing two WebGL canvases + big DOM). Not a code bug. The v16 QA passed in the same environment, so it's tolerable.</p><p><span class="font-semibold" data-streamdown="strong">My conclusion: the REAL bugs were:</span></p><ol class="list-inside list-decimal whitespace-normal" data-streamdown="ordered-list">
<li class="py-1 [&amp;&gt;p]:inline" data-streamdown="list-item">✅ FIXED: Blossom module race (lazy mount + blossom:ready event)</li>
<li class="py-1 [&amp;&gt;p]:inline" data-streamdown="list-item">Test artifacts: GPU stalls slowing animations + boundary-rounding scroll targets</li>
</ol><p><span class="font-semibold" data-streamdown="strong">Remaining REAL issues to verify/fix from probe data:</span></p><ul class="list-inside list-disc whitespace-normal" data-streamdown="unordered-list">
<li class="py-1 [&amp;&gt;p]:inline" data-streamdown="list-item">
<p>Probe2-C: scrolling back above the section while hold engaged — lenis STOPPED → the scrollTo back was IGNORED (lenis semantics) → the page was stuck at 12150 with the shot unfired, stage visible! <span class="font-semibold" data-streamdown="strong">A REAL USER WOULD BE STUCK</span>: hold stops lenis; user tries to scroll back UP (wheel up) — lenis stopped → wheel does nothing → user can't go back! They can only go forward (auto-fire via my wheel listener — which fires on ANY wheel incl. wheel-up! <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm" data-streamdown="inline-code">on("wheel", take)</code> — take() = autoFire — so scrolling up fires the shot! Then after the shot, lenis stays stopped until bloom → then started. OK so the user is never truly stuck — any interaction fires the shot. That's by design ("any scroll attempt fires the shot").</p>
<p>BUT what about the reverse-reset path: after the shot has fired and the user scrolls back ABOVE the section (onLeaveBack → cleanup + lenis.start() ✓) — probe2-C: lenis stayed STOPPED at 12150 because scrollTo was ignored while stopped. In REAL usage: user scrolls back up with wheel → my hold listeners... wait — at probe2-C, hold was ENGAGED (from B) but the shot never fired. The user scrolls up → wheel listener → take() → autoFire → shot → bloom → lenis.start(). So the escape hatch works via the shot. Fine — nobody gets stuck. (The scrollbar drag also → scroll event → but scroll won't move while lenis stopped... scrollbar drag moves NATIVE scroll directly! lenis stopped only blocks lenis's smooth handling, native scrollbar still moves the page → scroll event → my listener fires take() only if scrollY &gt; holdTop+60 — scrolling UP doesn't meet that... BUT onLeaveBack of the hold trigger fires when going back above 10300 → cleanup + lenis.start() ✓ (I added that). ✓ covered.)</p>
</li>
<li class="py-1 [&amp;&gt;p]:inline" data-streamdown="list-item">
<p>Probe3 line: <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm" data-streamdown="inline-code">scrollTo(12150)</code> while stopped → ignored — my onBloom does <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm" data-streamdown="inline-code">lenis.start()</code> THEN <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm" data-streamdown="inline-code">scrollTo</code> ✓ correct order.</p>
</li>
<li class="py-1 [&amp;&gt;p]:inline" data-streamdown="list-item">
<p>One more potential issue: in hold(), <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm" data-streamdown="inline-code">Shot.enter()</code> is called immediately when the pin starts — but the approach animation may still be settling (lenis animating to the pin start). Minor.</p>
</li>
<li class="py-1 [&amp;&gt;p]:inline" data-streamdown="list-item">
<p><span class="font-semibold" data-streamdown="strong">The cinema-mode edge</span>: cinema scrolls via <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm" data-streamdown="inline-code">scrollTo</code> native (gsap onUpdate → scrollTo(0, proxy.y)) — native scrollTo BYPASSES lenis → scroll moves even while stopped ✓ — and my hold's scroll listener fires take() when passing holdTop+60 ✓. But better: I already added the cinemaHint check → autoFire at 0.25s ✓.</p>
</li>
</ul><p>Now — one thing that probe2-B revealed: <span class="font-semibold" data-streamdown="strong">swBigOp "1" but I never fired the shot in probe2</span> — the wish text shows even without the shot (scrub is independent of the archery)! For a user who auto-fires or drags, fine — but the design intent: washes → tree → wish. If someone force-scrolls past the hold (scrollbar) WITHOUT the shot firing... the scroll listener take() fires the shot at +60px. OK — the shot always happens before deep progress. Except reduce-motion (skipShot — stage hidden, no shot) ✓ intended.</p><p>Hmm — BUT what if ScrollTrigger onEnter→hold happens and the user's wheel event does NOT fire take because... they use a trackpad with momentum? Any wheel/touchmove → take ✓.</p><p><span class="font-semibold" data-streamdown="strong">Another real issue from probe2-B</span>: <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm" data-streamdown="inline-code">stageOp: "1"</code> — at y=12150 (post-pin-start, scrub 0.68) the STAGE was visible?! In probe2, the shot never fired (shotFired false) — hold engaged at 10300 → Shot.enter() → stage visible ✓ expected (the archery is showing, waiting for the shot, while scroll is stopped at 12150 — the user force-scrolled). Visual: archery overlay ON TOP of the tree sequence (stage z3 &gt; canvas z2) — the heart+bow floating over the half-bloomed tree. Slightly odd but an edge case (user dragged scrollbar hard past the hold). The autoFire would trigger within 60px... it stopped at 12150 — wait why didn't the scroll listener fire take at 10360?? Because hold engaged AFTER the scroll finished at 12150 (the crossing happened during the stall-slowed animation; onEnter→hold→lenis.stop() — the remaining animation... hmm, but the "scroll" listener was registered inside hold() — if the animation had ALREADY reached 12150 before hold() ran, no further scroll events → no take. Then the user is at 12150 with archery visible + scroll stopped (lenis stopped → wh